e geometric invariants: topology, the metric, \nvarious notions of curvature, and relationships among these. \nThere are analytic invariants: eigenvalues of the Laplacian, \nwave equations, Schri:idinger equations. There are the invari-\nants that come from Hamiltonian mechanics: geodesic flow, \nergodic properties, periodic geodesics. Finally, there are impor-\ntant results relating different types of invariants. To keep the \nsize of this survey manageable, Berger focuses on five areas of \nRiemannian geometry: Curvature and topology; the construc-\ntion of and the classification of space forms; distinguished \nmetrics, especially Einstein metrics; eigenvalues and eigenfunc-\ntions of the Laplacian; the study of periodic geodesics and the \ngeodesic flow. Other topics are treated in less detail in a sepa-\nrate section. \nThis is the English translation of the \noriginal Japanese book. In this volume, \"Fermat's Dream\", core \ntheories in modern number theory are introduced. Develop-\nments are given in elliptic curves, p-adic numbers, the \ns -function, and the number fields. This work presents an \nelegant perspective on the wonder of numbers. Number Theory \n2 on class field theory, and Number Theory 3 on Iwasawa \ntheory and the theory of modular forms, are forthcoming in \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nISSN 0002-9920 (https://www.ams.org/journals/notices/200001/200001FullIssue.pdf)\nciteturn639search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Number Theory 1Fermat's Dream ... 14-XX, All AMS members $20, List $25, Order code\nPreviously Announced Publications \nSupplementary Reading \nNumber Theory 1 \nFermat's Dream \nKazuya Kato, University of Tokyo, japan, \nNobushige Kurokawa, Tokyo Institute of Technology, \njapan, and Takeshi Saito, University of Tokyo, japan \nIn the second part, the spectral theory of the differential oper-\nator L is developed by expressing L in the form L = T + S, \nwhere Tis the principal part determined by the nth order \nderivative and S is the part determined by the lower-order \nderivatives. The spectral theory of T is developed first using \noperator theory, and then the spectral theory of L is developed \nby treating L as a perturbation of T. Regular and irregular \nboundary values are allowed for T, and regular boundary \nvalues are considered for L. Special features of the spectral \ntheory for L and T include the foliowing: calculation of the \neigenvalues, algebraic multiplicities and ascents; calculation of \nthe associated family of projections which project onto the \ngeneralized eigenspaces; completeness of the generalized \neigenfunctions; uniform bounds on the family of all finite \nsums of the associated projections; and expansions of func-\ntions in series of generalized eigenfunctions of L and T. \nTl\"J.is is the English trru\"lslation of the original Japanese book. In \nthis volume, \"Fermat's Dream\", core theories in modern \nnumber theory are introduced. Developments are given in \nelliptic curves, p-adic numbers, the (-function, and the \nnumber fields. This work presents an elegant perspective on \nthe wonder of numbers. Number Theory 2 on class field \ntheory, and Number Theory 3 on Iwasawa theory and the \ntheory of modular forms, are forthcoming in the series. \nMathematical Surveys and Monographs, Volume 73 \nTranslations of Mathematical Monographs (Iwanami Series in \nModern Mathematics), Volume 186 \nOctober 1999, 252 pages, Hardcover, ISBN 0-8218-2049-4, \nLC 99-44328, 2000 Mathematics Subject Classification: 34105, \n47E05; 34B27, 34Ll0, 34120, 47A53, Individual member $39, \nList $65, Institutional member $52, Order code SURV/73RT001 \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIREP0203$10.xls (https://www.iitg.ac.in/lib/nbhmbook.pdf)\nciteturn639search14 [wordlim: 200] <tr><td>MB1346</td><td>Groups, languages and geometry: Proceedings</td><td>AMS-IMS-SIAM Joint Summ